      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "An 18-year-old female presented with acute posterior wall infarction after exercise stress. Coronary angiography showed an aneurysm of the proximal right coronary artery partially occluded with thrombi, followed by a complete occlusion of the vessel. The left coronary artery was normal. Despite immediate intracoronary thrombolysis she developed a large posterior wall necrosis. Angiographic follow-up revealed worsening left ventricular function but not progression of the aneurysmatic ectasia. The diagnosis congenital coronary artery aneurysm was made since there was no evidence for an atherosclerotic, infectious or inflammatory vascular disease. The patient was treated conservatively and within 2 years of follow-up the clinical course was uneventful."},
